<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.6.3: Finding Solutions to Unordered Sets of Equivalent Systems</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00463</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.6.3: Finding Solutions to Unordered Sets of Equivalent Systems</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ddda2b10-6a8e-47db-acd6-5d78bdcb1010</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Your teacher will give you <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/44bc8768abb54e8e3ed6544ec6108f910b72028d">some slips of paper</link> with systems of equations written on them. Each system represents a step in solving this system:
  
  \( \begin {cases}\frac45 x + 6y = 15\\ \text-x + 18y = 11 \end{cases} \)
</para>
<para>Arrange the slips in the order that would lead to a solution. Be prepared to:</para>
<!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->

<!--Q#-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Describe what move takes one system to the next system.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( \begin {cases}\frac45 x + 6y = 15\\ \text-x + 18y = 11 \end{cases} \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis></para>
    <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}4x + 30y = 75\\ \text-x + 18y = 11 \end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis></para>
    <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}4x + 30y = 75\\ \text-4x + 72y = 44 \end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis></para>
    <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}4x + 30y &amp;= \hspace{2mm}75\\ 102y &amp;=119 \end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis></para>
    <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}4x + 30y &amp;=75\\ y &amp;=\frac76 \end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis></para>
    <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}4x +35 &amp;=75\\ y &amp;=\frac76 \end{align}\end{cases} \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 6 - </emphasis></para>
    <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}4x &amp;=40\\ y &amp;=\frac76\end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 7 - </emphasis></para>
    <para>\( \begin {cases} \begin{align}x &amp;=10\\ y &amp;=\frac76\end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->

<!--Q#2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the original system and step 1.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <list>
      <item>Multiply the first equation by 5.</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->

<!--Q#3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the system in step 1 to the system in step 2.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a samples.</para>
    <list>
      <item>Multiply the second equation by 4.</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->

<!--Q#4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the system in step 2 to the system in step 3.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a samples.</para>
    <list>
      <item>Add the two equations in Step 2.</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->

<!--Q#5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the system in step 3 to the system in step 4.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a samples.</para>
    <list>
      <item>Divide each side of \( 102y=119 \) by 102 to solve for \( y \).</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->

<!--Q#6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the system in step 4 to the system in step 5.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a samples.</para>
    <list>
      <item>Substitute \( \frac{7}{6} \)  for y in the first equation and evaluating the expression.</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!--Q#7-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the system in step 5 to the system in step 6.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a samples.</para>
    <para>From Step 5 to Step 6: Subtract 35 from each side of the first equation.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!--Q#8-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain why the systems are equivalent between the system in step 6 to the system in step 7.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a samples.</para>
    <para>From Step 6 to Step 7: Divide each side of \( 4x = 40 \) by 4 to solve for \( x \).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->

